WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.010
So, I always think of calcium as a slightly
‘British element’ because the white cliffs

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.770
of Dover are made from calcium carbonate,
chalk.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:14.910
Calcium is one of the Group 2, or alkaline
earth elements, it has got an atomic number

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:16.470
of 20.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:22.770
Calcium itself is a metal but people most
commonly see it as compounds.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:29.070
It is a relatively soft, sort of silvery metal
which I can actually show you. This is quite

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:34.210
an old sample of calcium, ok, so it is a bit
tarnished. Calcium is very, very reactive

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.899
so it quickly forms a layer. So you can see
it is kind of shining right there. It quickly

00:00:38.899 --> 00:00:43.050
forms a layer of sort of oxides and nitrides
in the air which is what this calcium has

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.440
done here.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:51.299
So many of you will have come across calcium
sulphate which is used as chalk in school,

00:00:51.299 --> 00:00:57.379
nowadays people use dry markers and things
but there is still, we sometimes use chalk

00:00:57.379 --> 00:00:59.109
on blackboards.

00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:03.640
The phrase ‘in the limelight’, which I
am sure you have heard of you know, being

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.240
in television, the phrase ‘in the limelight’
actually comes from the fact that they used

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.830
to use calcium oxides and they used to aim
a very hot flame at it and make it sort of

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:17.220
incandescent and that is how the theatres
and sort of music halls used to be lit so

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.540
when you were in the limelight, you literally
were in a light of lime and funny enough we

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.320
use electricity now for it and for lighting
all these sorts of things but the phrase still

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.960
lasts which is quite nice.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:35.140
And the compound of calcium and carbon so
called calcium carbide reacts with water to

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:42.050
produce acetylene, a very reactive compound
of carbon, which burns with a bright light

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:48.640
and in the early days of motoring, and cycling,
people use calcium carbide lights in which

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:53.890
they had some of this powdered calcium carbide
at the bottom of the lamp and water dripped

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:59.470
slowly on it to produce a gas which burnt
and gave a beam which was not nearly as bright

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:03.410
as one of our current headlights but was still
bright enough to get around.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:08.140
And actually sort of expanding the chemistry
of this compound, this element, I think which

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:12.049
is slightly misunderstood and sometimes thought
of as being a bit boring, but I don’t think,

00:02:12.049 --> 00:02:15.670
it is not boring, I think it will have a great
chemistry and hopefully very useful.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:22.610
Well us chemists like to see spectacular things,
some things do cool things, I mean the transition

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:26.299
metals change colour you know, which is you
know, you have all these nice colours of transitions

00:02:26.299 --> 00:02:31.190
metals, but the things that are sort of in
the main group which I mean S blocks the ones

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:35.409
on the left and the P block the one on the
right, don’t tend to form very coloured

00:02:35.409 --> 00:02:39.870
compounds, it is a particular thing to the
transition metals, most of the time. So they

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:43.450
don’t do much in the way of colour change,
a bit boring. I mean most calcium compounds

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:48.650
are white. I mean ok, your bones and your
teeth are white aren't they really, but still,

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:54.340
we can do interesting chemistry with them,
you can make calcium compounds that can be

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:58.909
used as reaction catalysts which would be
great because calcium is a cheap, pretty benign

00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:03.319
as well, so it is an ideal catalyst compared
to a lot of the expensive transition metals.

